WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.310
While a number of talented actors have given
us their own takes on Gotham's Clown Prince

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:08.720
of Crime, it's what we don't know about the
character that makes his presence so intriguing

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:09.620
and terrifying.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.920
Some fans have tried to fill in those gaps
themselves, and the results are both absurd

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.080
and horrifying much like the Joker himself.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.400
"Why so serious?"

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.920
Tim Burton's 1989 Batman not only heralded
a new era in well-made, serious comic book

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.520
movies, but it also offered a major reimagining
of a huge part of the Caped Crusader mythos.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.660
The event that sent Bruce Wayne on the path
to becoming Batman: the night his parents

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:38.300
were robbed and killed by an otherwise petty
criminal named Joe Chill.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:42.500
In the world of the '89 cinematic Batman,
however, the Joker is responsible for the

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.670
ignoble end of Thomas and Martha Wayne.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:49.120
That reveal only increased Bruce Wayne's loathing
of the Joker, which motivated him to stop

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:52.760
this criminal menace, and helped elevate the
Joker to arch-villain status.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:57.290
Or, per one fan theory pertaining specifically
to Burton's Batman, what if the Joker was

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:01.460
the same Joker from most other Batman media,
and wasn't the guy who killed the Waynes?

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.760
Adherents to this theory say that since Batman
is so obviously mentally and emotionally tortured,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.880
he projects blame for the death of his parents
onto the Joker because that's the guy he's

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:10.380
fighting.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.720
The Dark Knight merely planted the Joker connection
into his memory in order to explain his own

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:15.860
hatred for the guy.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.640
"You think you're my greatest enemy?"

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:18.140
"Yes!

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.760
You're obsessed with me."

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:21.680
"No I'm not.  Yes you are."

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.100
"No I'm not.  Yes you are."

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.470
While some theories seem to balance on the
fine line between fan fiction and canon, others

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:29.740
are…a little more out there.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.280
Strap in kiddies, because we're in for a bumpy
ride.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.790
Remember that adorably creepy kid in The Shining?

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:38.940
Well, he wound up being the main character
in Stephen King's sequel Doctor Sleep.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:44.000
But that came out in 2013, a full five years
after The Dark Knight in 2008.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.180
So some fans developed an...alternative theory.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.140
What if Danny Torrance grew up to be...the
Joker?

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:55.220
One anonymous meme creator postulated just
that, using the first of Joker's many stated

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.500
origin stories as evidence.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:01.140
"My father was a drinker.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.980
And one night, he goes off crazier than usual.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.610
Mommy gets the kitchen knife to defend herself.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:10.840
He doesn't like that."

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:14.660
Now, we're not seriously arguing that The
Dark Knight was meant to be a sequel to The

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:19.960
Shining, but maybe Christopher Nolan's interpretation
of the character was partially inspired by

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.420
the 1980 horror classic.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:26.180
Nolan is a huge fan of Shining director Stanley
Kubrick so much so that he helped restore

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.280
Kubrick's 2001: A Space Odyssey.

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:32.800
And there's precedent, as Jack Nicholson's
performance as Jack Torrance did help secure

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.460
him the role of the Joker in 1989's Batman.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.540
So hey, anything's possible, right?

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:39.840
"You out of your f---ing mind?"

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:44.720
He looks like the Joker, he acts like the
Joker, and the movie's named after the Joker,

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:48.760
but according to some fans, Joaquin Phoenix
isn't playing the Joker in Joker.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.840
At least, not the Joker we all know and love.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:53.800
For starters, Phoenix is playing a character
named Arthur Fleck.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.500
While we've heard Joker's name as "Jack Napier"
before, most notably in Tim Burton's Batman,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.330
as well as Batman: The Animated Series, he's
never had a single, 100% canonical origin

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:03.980
story.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:08.500
And except for all the Joker trappings, the
character of Arthur Fleck is a whole new creation.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.800
"When you bring me out can you introduce me
as 'Joker?'"

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:17.580
Some fans have theorized that the Joker Arthur
becomes isn't the Joker that Batman fans know

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.920
at all, and that his taking on the name of
Joker is a big red herring.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.680
Mr. Fleck is also several decades older than
the boy who would become Batman.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.540
Phoenix is in his 40s, while Dante Pereira-Olson,
who has been cast as "Young Bruce Wayne,"

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:33.690
is very young indeed, making it impossible
to imagine these two growing up to have any

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:34.620
kind of rivalry.

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:39.380
This has caused fans to speculate that Phoenix's
Arthur Fleck is not the real Joker, but his

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:44.130
clownish criminal predecessor, who inspires
the true Joker years later, Others have floated

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:48.320
a different theory that Arthur's Joker just
doesn't age, or die, at all.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:53.160
Despite its sometimes realistic take on crime,
Batman is still about an oddly dressed billionaire

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.180
vigilante who tracks down weirdo, theatrical
villains.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.240
Like a guy in a question-mark-covered one-piece,
and an umbrella-wielding penguin man.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.500
In other words, it also wouldn't be completely
absurd if the Joker, everybody's favorite

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.340
crazed criminal clown, turned out to be immortal.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:11.420
But don't take our word for it this is actually
something that's been explored in the comics.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:17.340
According to Batman #37, released in 2014
as part of the "Endgame" arc, the Joker can

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:18.760
and will live forever.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:23.360
In the comic, Commissioner Gordon uncovers
old newspaper photos depicting the Joker committing

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.020
crimes in 1910 and 1946.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.639
He assumes the Joker doctored the pictures,
but after the Joker endures a seemingly fatal

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.700
gunshot blast, it would seem the trickster
is telling the truth.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.259
By the end of "Endgame," both Batman and the
Joker lay dead, but then maybe the Joker just

00:04:38.259 --> 00:04:42.159
wants everybody to think he's dead, since
that's definitely a mistake Gotham City has

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:42.960
made before.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.460
"I think you and I are destined to do this
forever."

